-          "quote": "Fix one mode $m$ and a positive-length closed trial interval $J=[a,b]$ with $a<b$. **Certified hybrid event-enclosure data** consist of the following.\n\n1. A continuous piecewise dense reconstruction $U$ accumulated from the last reported reset through the current trial prefix, a finite rational residual-tube certificate for the mode ODE launched from that reported reset whose stored reconstruction is exactly $U$, and its returned `certified` status. Write $\\eta_{\\rm loc}$ for the rational upper bound on the checker-certified distance from $U$ to that exact mode solution over the entire prefix. The soundness interface is [[def-validated-residual-tube-certificate]], [[alg-validated-residual-tube-certificate-checker]], and [[thm-validated-residual-tube-certificate-sound]]. One concrete dense-output formula is [[def-heun-quadratic-dense-output]], but any reconstruction with such a certificate is allowed.\n2. A rational **reference error bound** $\\eta_{\\rm ref}\\ge0$ and a finite error ledger that justifies $\\|y_{\\rm ref}(t)-U(t)\\|_2\\le\\eta_{\\rm ref}$ throughout the common mode prefix, where $y_{\\rm ref}$ is the exact incoming-mode continuation of the reference hybrid trajectory. At the last reported reset time, both trajectories are in the same new mode and the ledger contains an upper bound $E$ on their reset-state discrepancy. If $L$ is a state-Lipschitz bound and $H$ the elapsed duration through the current prefix, the ledger sets $\\eta_{\\rm ref}=\\eta_{\\rm loc}+e^{LH}E$. When a no-event trial is accepted, append its dense panel to $U$, extend the residual-tube certificate, and update $H$ and $\\eta_{\\rm loc}$ while retaining $E$. When the prefix ends at a transverse event with guard residual bound $\\varepsilon$, guard state-Lipschitz bound $L_g$, crossing margin $\\nu$, incoming and outgoing field bounds $M^-,M^+$, reset state-Lipschitz bound $L_R$, and reset time-Lipschitz bound $L_{R,t}$, it records $\\Delta_{\\rm next}=(L_g\\eta_{\\rm ref}+\\varepsilon)/\\nu$ and $E_{\\rm next}=L_R\\eta_{\\rm ref}+(L_RM^-+L_{R,t}+M^+)\\Delta_{\\rm next}$. The $M^+\\Delta_{\\rm next}$ term accounts for the exact trajectory's outgoing flow between its true event time and the reported upper event-box endpoint. The local residual-tube status in item 1 alone does not establish this total reference bound; prior reset-state errors must be included.\n3. For each active transition $(m,n)$, a state-Lipschitz constant $L_g$ for its guard on the enlarged reference tube, interval enclosures $G^U_{mn}(I)$ of $g_{mn}(t,U(t))$, and finite interval evaluators $E_{mn}(I),D_{mn}(I)$ for every queried closed subinterval $I\\subseteq J$. The evaluators must contain, respectively, $g_{mn}(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))$ and $\\partial_tg_{mn}(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))+D_yg_{mn}(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))f_m(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))$ for every $t\\in I$; $G^U_{mn}(I)$ must contain $g_{mn}(t,U(t))$. By [[lem-hybrid-guard-derivative-along-mode]], the second enclosure is the derivative of the exact reference guard. For orientation $\\sigma_{mn}$, a bracket has certified transverse margin $\\nu>0$ when $\\sigma_{mn}D_{mn}(I)\\subseteq[\\nu,\\infty)$. Reset data include state and time Lipschitz constants $L_R,L_{R,t}$ and bounds $M^-,M^+$ on the incoming and outgoing mode-field norms over the event-time bracket.\n\nThe interval enclosures and the reference error ledger are admissible only when their stated inclusions and error bounds are justified. A dense-output point evaluation alone establishes none of them. If a required evaluator, ledger entry, or inclusion is unavailable, the event data are uncertified and no event-absence conclusion is permitted.\n",
+          "quote": "Fix one mode $m$ and a positive-length closed trial interval $J=[a,b]$ with $a<b$, contained in a finite accumulated prefix that starts at the last reported reset. **Certified hybrid event-enclosure data** consist of the following.\n\n1. A continuous piecewise-$C^1$ dense reconstruction $U$ on the accumulated prefix, the exact mode solution $y_{\\rm loc}$ launched from the last reported reset on that prefix, and a finite bound $\\eta_{\\rm loc}\\ge0$ justified to satisfy $\\|y_{\\rm loc}(t)-U(t)\\|_2\\le\\eta_{\\rm loc}$ throughout the prefix. The provider also supplies a finite bound $\\eta_{\\rm ref}\\ge0$ justified to satisfy $\\|y_{\\rm ref}(t)-U(t)\\|_2\\le\\eta_{\\rm ref}$ there, where $y_{\\rm ref}$ is the exact incoming-mode continuation of the reference hybrid trajectory. On an open interval containing each queried trial interval, this reference continuation exists and is $C^1$, so each exact reference guard is $C^1$ there. Its finite error ledger records the aligned mode-segment start and the discrepancy $E$ between the reported and reference states there. One sufficient ledger calculation, when both exact mode solutions remain in a common convex tube and the field is $L$-Lipschitz in state there for duration $H$, is $\\eta_{\\rm ref}=\\eta_{\\rm loc}+e^{LH}E$ by [[lem-ode-flow-lipschitz-on-a-tube]]. A provider may instead supply a separately justified total bound $\\eta_{\\rm ref}$ directly. The rational route in [[def-validated-residual-tube-certificate]], [[alg-validated-residual-tube-certificate-checker]], and [[thm-validated-residual-tube-certificate-sound]] is one sufficient provider for $\\eta_{\\rm loc}$: its panel-radius functions must be accompanied by a finite uniform upper bound on the whole prefix. It is restricted to the rational-expression fields and polynomial reconstructions stated there; it is not required. One concrete dense-output formula is [[def-heun-quadratic-dense-output]], which by itself is not an error certificate.\n2. For each active transition $(m,n)$, a state-Lipschitz constant $L_g$ for its guard on a tube containing the exact and reconstructed states, interval enclosures $G^U_{mn}(I)$ of $g_{mn}(t,U(t))$, and finite interval evaluators $E_{mn}(I),D_{mn}(I)$ for every queried closed subinterval $I\\subseteq J$. For every $t\\in I$, $E_{mn}(I)$ must contain $g_{mn}(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))$, $D_{mn}(I)$ must contain $\\partial_tg_{mn}(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))+D_yg_{mn}(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))f_m(t,y_{\\rm ref}(t))$, and $G^U_{mn}(I)$ must contain $g_{mn}(t,U(t))$. By [[lem-hybrid-guard-derivative-along-mode]], the second enclosure is the derivative of the exact reference guard. For orientation $\\sigma_{mn}$, an interval has certified transverse margin $\\nu>0$ when $\\sigma_{mn}D_{mn}(I)\\subseteq[\\nu,\\infty)$. Reset data include state and time Lipschitz constants $L_R,L_{R,t}$ and bounds $M^-,M^+$ on the incoming and outgoing mode-field norms over the event-time bracket.\n3. A finite error-ledger update rule and a certified separation bound $\\delta>0$ between consecutive selected exact hybrid event times on the horizon. The provider must supply a finite sound justification or validation for every successor local or total error bound it returns. When a no-event trial is accepted, append its dense panel to $U$, retain the aligned-start discrepancy $E$, and certify valid local and total reference-error bounds over the enlarged prefix. When a prefix ends at a selected transverse event, let $\\tau$ be the unique selected exact event time in the certified physical-time bracket and let $\\widehat\\tau$ be the bracket's upper endpoint. Suppose the whole bracket has certified signed derivative margin $\\nu>0$, the dense guard residual at $\\widehat\\tau$ is bounded by $\\varepsilon$, the total reference error on the bracket is bounded by $\\eta_{\\rm ref}$, the reset has state and time Lipschitz constants $L_R,L_{R,t}$, and the incoming and outgoing fields have norm bounds $M^-,M^+$ on the intervals required for comparison. A sufficient conditional provider rule is to justify $0\\le\\widehat\\tau-\\tau\\le\\Delta_{\\rm next}$ with $\\Delta_{\\rm next}=(L_g\\eta_{\\rm ref}+\\varepsilon)/\\nu$, require $\\Delta_{\\rm next}<\\delta/3$, and set $E_{\\rm next}=L_R\\eta_{\\rm ref}+(L_RM^-+L_{R,t}+M^+)\\Delta_{\\rm next}$ for the exact reset evaluated at the reported state. If the reported reset is computed as $\\widehat R$ with a separately certified error $\\|\\widehat R-R\\|_2\\le\\varepsilon_R$, add $\\varepsilon_R$ to this $E_{\\rm next}$ bound; exact reset evaluation has $\\varepsilon_R=0$. The provider must validate the hypotheses and inequalities and certify the next prefix's total reference-error bound with this aligned-start discrepancy included (or provide another sound proof of that total bound). Since $\\widehat\\tau-\\tau\\le\\Delta_{\\rm next}<\\delta/3<\\delta$, the next selected exact event cannot precede $\\widehat\\tau$. These formulas are a sufficient provider-supplied rule, not inequalities proved by this definition itself. If any proof obligation, required bound, separation certificate, or strict lag test is missing or invalid, no restart ledger update is certified. A local error bound alone does not establish the total reference bound; prior reset-state errors must be included in $\\eta_{\\rm ref}$.\n\nThe provider may be any finite, sound method that supplies the stated bounds and interval inclusions; it returns `certified` with the data or `inconclusive` without them. Its checks may be provider-specific: a rational residual-tube checker is a sufficient concrete branch, not a universal evaluator for arbitrary $C^1$ fields or reset states. The bounds and enclosures are admissible only when their claimed inequalities and inclusions are justified. A dense-output point evaluation alone establishes none of them. If a required evaluator, ledger entry, bound, or inclusion is unavailable or nonfinite, the event data are uncertified and no event-absence conclusion is permitted.",
